so bear with me.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:15.350
So, you know in films,
there are different actors

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:17.550
with different roles, right?

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.930
And basically, the movies are created

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:21.420
as a result of the interaction

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:22.920
of these different actors.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:26.380
And each actors play
their different roles.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.720
And it is very similar,

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.930
actually ourselves, our organs are formed

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:35.530
from a small functional
units called cells,

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:37.590
and have different cell types,

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:39.600
they have different functions.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.700
And by interacting with each other,

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.160
they form our body,

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.570
and they form our organs.

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:46.680
And stem cells,

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:50.090
they are like an actor
who can play any role.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:51.733
So the stem cells,

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:57.120
it's the kind of actor who
already have the script,

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.120
who's already familiar with this role.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:01.290
And they can just go in the movie

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:02.976
and play the parts

00:16:02.976 --> 00:16:04.823
when they get the signal.

00:16:04.823 --> 00:16:07.300
So stem cells are type of cells,

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:08.900
they can transform into,

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:12.003
we say differentiated
into different cell types.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.240
And my laboratory,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.383
we are working on stem
cells found in the skin.

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:23.343
So today I want to
introduce to you the skin

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:28.670
and talk more about the
stem cells in the skin.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:32.130
So I'm gonna share my
screen to show some slides

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:35.720
with the cartoons of skin.

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:41.020
I think you need to enable me to...

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:45.313
Thank you.

00:16:57.703 --> 00:17:01.120
Okay, so this is how our skin looks like.

00:17:03.010 --> 00:17:04.400
This is always like cartoon.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:07.410
Hopefully today, we will
also see with our own eyes

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:08.620
how it looks like.

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:11.740
But it has different compartments.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:14.663
So can you also see the mouse?

00:17:16.820 --> 00:17:18.630
- [Lizzie] Yes.
- Okay cool.

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:22.690
So this upper compartment
is called epidermis.

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:24.606
So this is basically what see

00:17:24.606 --> 00:17:25.970
when we look at our arms.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.717
This is the outer part of our skin,

00:17:28.717 --> 00:17:30.970
and it is called epidermis.

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:32.189
And under the epidermis,

00:17:32.189 --> 00:17:35.720
there is this part which is called dermis.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:39.050
And dermis has its own cell types,

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:43.273
and also there are blood
vessels, and also neurons.

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:46.070
And the other compartment

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:47.080
that we can actually see

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:50.990
with our eyes is the hair follicles.

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:54.610
And similar to epidermis and dermis,

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:58.620
hair follicles are also made
from different types of cells.

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:03.620
So this is the big picture
of how our skin looks like.

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:06.650
Now we're gonna zoom in
to the epidermis part.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:11.880
So here, as you can see,

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:13.660
our epidermis actually,

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:15.270
when we look from outside,

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:17.360
it doesn't seem very complex,

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:19.850
but actually, it's a very complex tissue.

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:22.620
It forms from different layers.

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:24.220
Here in the basal layer,

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.540
we actually have our stem cells.

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:28.240
And in the other layers,

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:31.260
we have different type of cells.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:32.820
And as I mentioned earlier,

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:35.130
this stem cells in the basal layer,

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:40.130
they can differentiate
into the other cell types.

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:45.609
So I want to ask you again

00:18:45.609 --> 00:18:48.850
in a very fun poll question.

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:51.620
There is no right or wrong answer,

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:53.939
just to interact with each other.

00:18:53.939 --> 00:18:57.733
Do you know why the stem
cells might be important?

00:18:58.989 --> 00:19:02.320
Or why do you think we're
working on stem cells,

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:05.120
we are trying to understand their biology?

00:19:46.667 --> 00:19:50.084
I'm just going to wait a little bit more.

00:20:01.527 --> 00:20:03.610
So most of you said both,

00:20:04.913 --> 00:20:06.580
and that is correct.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:11.400
So yes, stem cells,

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:16.367
since they have this property

00:20:16.367 --> 00:20:19.470
of making a different cell types,

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:23.030
they can actually regenerate a tissue.

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:28.030
And also, they play role in
certain skin disease as well.

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:30.920
So here now, I think though,

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:32.843
I will start with wound healing

00:20:32.843 --> 00:20:37.400
because I think this is
one of the great examples

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:41.490
of how some cells regenerate a tissue.

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:46.490
So I think probably a lot of
you can relate with the wounds.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:48.860
And as you all know that
when you have wounds,

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:51.210
it actually heals pretty quickly,

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:53.140
and that's kind of awesome.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:56.610
And what happens is
actually when we have wounds

00:20:56.610 --> 00:21:01.160
or we have an open area
in our skin, right?

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:03.007
And we haven't talked about it,

00:21:03.007 --> 00:21:05.560
but the main role of our skin is

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:08.000
it is actually our protective organ.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.080
It protects us from infections

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:15.780
or invasion of the microbes
outside our body, right?

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:18.160
And when we have this open wounds,

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:22.580
actually, we are exposed
to those infections.

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:24.270
So when there is this wounding,

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.780
actually our stem cells,

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:27.750
they sense this,

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:30.040
and they become activated.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:33.850
So first, they actually start
to make more stem cells.

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:36.920
And then, they migrate through,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:41.490
move through this wounded area.

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:44.630
And then they start making
different cell types.

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:47.390
So in this case, I said, for example,

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:51.900
stem cells are like an
actor who can play any role.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:54.610
And when they are getting signal,

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:57.240
they play a part in the movie.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:58.860
Like in this case, for example,

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:00.950
this signals is these wounds.

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:01.850
When there is wound,

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:04.460
now they're like, "Okay, I am needed."

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:07.770
And now, they start to
make different cell types.

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:11.232
And there are actually chronic ones.

00:22:11.232 --> 00:22:14.220
And some people have a chronic ones.

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:18.300
These are type of wounds, it doesn't heal.

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:21.630
So they actually need to get treatments.

00:22:23.835 --> 00:22:25.803
And there is, for example, other cases,

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.470
for example, if we have a small burn,

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:29.787
that's easy to heal,

00:22:29.787 --> 00:22:32.770
but we can also have the big burns,

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:35.490
like first degree burns.

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:38.280
And those are much difficult to recover.

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:43.280
So now, if we understand
how the stem cells work,

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:45.270
if we understand their biology,

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:47.980
we can actually find treatment

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:52.370
for chronic wounds or this kind
of large burns, skin burns.

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:55.433
And this is actually something
called stem cell therapy.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:02.480
And the other thing as you
already know is stem cells

00:23:04.993 --> 00:23:07.140
play role in diseases as well.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:10.160
There are many different
type of skin diseases.

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:13.270
And one of them is for
example, skin cancers.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:17.440
And my lab is actually
working on both wound healing

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:19.900
and also skin cancers.

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:24.120
So basically what cancer
is when there's stem cells,

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:26.490
they go out of control.

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:29.170
So actually, some people refer for example

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:32.280
skin cancer as non-healing wounds.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:34.730
And the reason is that in wounds,

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.410
stem cells start to make more stem cells

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:38.533
and other cells pods

00:23:38.533 --> 00:23:40.970
because they are needed, right?

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:42.470
But in the case of cancer,

00:23:42.470 --> 00:23:46.200
even if they are not needed in that area,

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.470
they start to proliferate

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:51.430
which means they make more stem cells

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:54.333
and then they form this tumors.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.100
And in some cases,

00:23:57.100 --> 00:23:59.410
the stem cells actually gained,

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:00.270
not just stem cell,

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:02.660
this transform cancer cells,

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:07.660
they gain the ability to escape from skin.

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:11.670
And as I showed in the initial slides,

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:12.690
under the skin,

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:15.320
there is dermis and
there are blood vessels.

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:18.340
And if they extend from the epidermis,

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:21.030
they can actually go into a blood vessel,

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:24.060
and this cancer cells can spread

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.230
to other organs and form tumors

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:28.143
in other organs as well.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:30.910
And how this...

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:33.863
Sorry.

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:39.110
And how these cells are cancerous

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:41.180
as stem cells go out of control,

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:45.829
and they transform into tumor cells.

00:24:45.829 --> 00:24:49.370
It's still under investigation.

00:24:49.370 --> 00:24:51.790
As scientists, we're
still trying to understand

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:53.540
how this is happening.

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:55.313
So one thing is, for example,

00:24:56.608 --> 00:25:00.250
UV lights can cause this transformation

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:03.460
if they expose our skin to UV light

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:06.240
for a very long time,

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:10.280
which is the sun is
emitting UV light as well

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:12.123
even if we can't see them.

00:25:12.123 --> 00:25:15.970
Then it may cause the
transformation of the cells

00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:17.520
to cancerous cells,

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:20.120
the normal cells to cancer cells.

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:21.540
And that's kind of why we need

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:25.653
to wear sunscreen creams
to protect our body.

